  "rai:dataLimitations": "The scene descriptions and agent distributions are generated by AI. It may not indicate any real world scenarios.",
  "rai:dataBiases": "We sampled only public areas with a large crowd of people; We also strategically selected rarer scenarios such as violence and escaping, that are underrepresented in the real world.",
  "rai:dataUseCases": "This map is used as an input for text-to-trajectory generation. The model could use only the description or if other metadata is needed.",
  "rai:dataSocialImpact": "As STRIDE-Bench is used for text-to-trajectory generation, it could facilitate in urban planning, venue safety auditing, and emergency preparedness.",
  "prov:wasGeneratedBy": [
    {
      "@type": "prov:Activity",
      "prov:type": {
        "@id": "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q4929239"
      },
      "prov:label": "Scenerio generation",
      "sc:description": "We adopt Berlonghi's typology of 11 crowd categories~\\cite{berlonghi1995understanding}. For each category, we prompt GPT-5.1 with a structured template that elicits: (i) a natural-language scenario description, (ii) candidate exits and event-center coordinates in the map pixel grid, and (iii) the initial crowd size and spatial distribution. Fields (ii) and (iii) provide a common initialization across models, allowing us to isolate context-conditioned behavior from setup variability. Prompts are provided in Appendix~\\ref{app:scenario-corpus}.\n\\textbf{Filtering and stratified sampling.}\nWe remove scenarios with insufficient agent density, exits or event centers that fall on obstacles, and near-duplicate descriptions. Obstacle conflicts are a known failure mode of polygon approximation, while duplicates are identified by cosine-similarity filtering over sentence embeddings. We also conduct a manual pass to remove residual map-realism failures.\nFrom the filtered pool, we perform \\emph{stratified oversampling} toward safety-critical categories, such as Escaping, Violent, and Dense. These categories are systematically underrepresented in real-world trajectory datasets~\\cite{pellegrini2009you, lerner2007crowds, robicquet2016learning}, since such dynamics cannot be ethically staged. They are also the regimes where context-conditioned generation has the highest applied value, including urban planning, venue safety auditing, and emergency preparedness. For each retained scenario, we prompt GPT-5.2 to generate the behavioral questions, measurements, and expected answers. Prompts are provided in Appendix~\\ref{app:qa-generation}.\n\\textbf{Calibration.}\nTo improve the human alignment of expected answers, we incorporate real-world crowd reference data into our human-trajectory knowledge base, \\emph{TrajFacts}. These references include events such as the Love Parade~\\cite{zhao2020assessing, krausz2012loveparade} and the Itaewon crowd crush~\\cite{hwang2025virtual}. \\emph{TrajFacts} provides reference values that help the model produce more realistic expected-answer ranges. Appendix~\\ref{app:gt-library} gives additional details.\nIn total, STRIDE-Bench contains 936 scenarios, 6,633 questions, and 11,696 measurements. Figure~\\ref{fig:benchmark-stats} shows the heatmap of DMT function usage across crowd categories."
    }
